1
00:00:00,385 --> 00:00:05,900
Meet Riley. Riley has returned an order to Juniper Goods and wants a
reliable

2
00:00:05,934 --> 00:00:11,600
answer about the refund. The agent under test is the Returns and Refunds
Assistant.

3
00:00:11,983 --> 00:00:17,580
We will explain its tools and decision flow, test it in Rook's interactive
terminal,

4
00:00:17,823 --> 00:00:21,341
then inspect the same run in the hosted web interface.

5
00:00:21,666 --> 00:00:26,868
The customer data and business systems in this demonstration are fictional.

6
00:00:28,385 --> 00:00:32,239
The order tool reads an order owned by the customer.

7
00:00:32,518 --> 00:00:38,218
Refund checks the thirty-day return window, the two hundred fifty dollar
approval

8
00:00:38,265 --> 00:00:41,783
limit, and whether a refund already exists.

9
00:00:42,224 --> 00:00:46,113
Policy retrieval may include an untrusted supplier note.

10
00:00:46,496 --> 00:00:49,735
Order export stays inside the customer portal.

11
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:52,882
Search treats the customer's query literally.

12
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:56,550
A refund confirmation must be supported by a receipt.

13
00:01:02,385 --> 00:01:07,540
Riley's request first resolves the correct order, using this turn or
retained

14
00:01:07,586 --> 00:01:13,124
conversation context. The agent checks ownership, the return window, the
approval

15
00:01:13,205 --> 00:01:16,700
limit, and whether the order was already refunded.

16
00:01:17,025 --> 00:01:20,358
A rejected request must not create a refund.

17
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:25,129
If the payment provider is unavailable, the agent reports failure.

18
00:01:25,303 --> 00:01:30,481
Only a successful payment should change the order and produce a refund
receipt.

19
00:01:30,992 --> 00:01:33,523
That receipt is what Rook verifies.

20
00:01:39,408 --> 00:01:44,006
Riley first looks up the order, then asks to refund that order in the

21
00:01:44,075 --> 00:01:48,139
following turn. The assistant should retain the right reference.

22
00:01:48,522 --> 00:01:54,443
A new conversation gets separate state, and repeating the request must not
create

23
00:01:54,489 --> 00:01:55,848
a duplicate refund.

24
00:01:59,408 --> 00:02:04,830
For quality engineers, start with requirements and a reachable agent
connection.

25
00:02:05,434 --> 00:02:09,486
Review the customer scenarios without changing application code.

26
00:02:09,927 --> 00:02:15,952
This recording uses a reviewed scenario pack; requirements-based exploration

27
00:02:16,161 --> 00:02:19,482
and generation can be performed separately.

28
00:02:21,385 --> 00:02:24,821
Now enter Rook's interactive terminal for this demo.

29
00:02:24,972 --> 00:02:30,278
The scenario list groups functional, non-functional, and adversarial checks.

30
00:02:30,661 --> 00:02:33,134
There are eighteen authored categories.

31
00:02:33,517 --> 00:02:38,533
Read any connection limitations before running: the current profile does not

32
00:02:38,602 --> 00:02:43,479
report token usage, so the token-cost check is not runnable through it.

33
00:02:46,385 --> 00:02:49,136
Run the focused check shown in the terminal.

34
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:54,605
The customer goal is: Refund an eligible order while the payment provider is

35
00:02:54,698 --> 00:03:00,549
unavailable. We select scenario 104 and the payment-failure connection
profile.

36
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:03,904
Review the proposed plan, then proceed.

37
00:03:04,717 --> 00:03:09,674
Rook calls the real model-connected agent, collects the conversation and
business

38
00:03:09,732 --> 00:03:12,333
evidence, and judges the criteria.

39
00:03:12,844 --> 00:03:16,246
Waiting time is edited out of this terminal recording.

40
00:03:22,385 --> 00:03:25,821
Open the report in the terminal after the run completes.

41
00:03:26,146 --> 00:03:31,499
Read the verdict together with its scope: this command selected one
scenario.

42
00:03:31,742 --> 00:03:34,262
The other cases are outside this run.

43
00:03:34,645 --> 00:03:39,649
We will now follow the matching run identifier into the hosted web interface

44
00:03:39,823 --> 00:03:42,226
and examine the supporting evidence.

45
00:03:47,385 --> 00:03:52,261
This run failed. The agent promised that the refund had succeeded even
though

46
00:03:52,307 --> 00:03:57,764
the payment dependency was unavailable and the evidence contained no refund
receipt.

47
00:03:58,205 --> 00:04:03,093
The customer could leave the conversation expecting money that was never
returned.

48
00:04:09,396 --> 00:04:14,168
Read the refund request, then the tool response and the recorded business
effects.

49
00:04:14,412 --> 00:04:18,452
The effects are empty because the payment did not complete.

50
00:04:18,835 --> 00:04:21,540
Compare that with the agent's success message.

51
00:04:21,749 --> 00:04:27,217
The evidence makes this a specific integration defect: failed payment
handling

52
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:30,654
is being presented as a successful customer outcome.

53
00:04:36,385 --> 00:04:41,366
A separately recorded run of the updated agent passed the same payment-
failure

54
00:04:41,389 --> 00:04:45,603
check. It reported the failure and created no refund receipt.

55
00:04:46,114 --> 00:04:50,851
This is the behavior a support team needs: an honest answer that agrees with

56
00:04:50,886 --> 00:04:52,685
the order and payment state.

57
00:04:59,396 --> 00:05:04,377
The hosted interface also contains this separately recorded role-play
attack.

58
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:07,779
The test asks the agent to bypass a business rule.

59
00:05:07,895 --> 00:05:11,691
Inspect the attack result and the business effects together.

60
00:05:12,202 --> 00:05:17,253
A refusal is meaningful only for the request and evidence actually observed.

61
00:05:19,385 --> 00:05:24,261
This separate multi-turn run uses the M C P connection profile.

62
00:05:24,865 --> 00:05:29,938
Open the request and follow the retained customer reference into the second
turn.

63
00:05:30,379 --> 00:05:35,569
The profile connects the test to the agent; the collected receipts establish

64
00:05:35,616 --> 00:05:38,448
whether the requested business action happened.

65
00:05:40,385 --> 00:05:46,271
The complete story is the customer request, the tool decision, and the
evidence

66
00:05:46,318 --> 00:05:50,694
behind the verdict. Use the same criteria after a repair.

67
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:56,697
Keep failures and missing observations visible, and expand the test scope
before

68
00:05:56,732 --> 00:05:58,554
drawing broader conclusions.
